<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Different Features of a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01092</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Different Features of a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>86115007-196b-4490-b3f8-434df9c68a76</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up</title>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Examine the following 4 graphs and think about why 1 of the graphs does not belong in the same group as the other 3.</para>
          
           <table class="unnumbered" id="table-00001" summary="This table presents various mathematical graph examples, including linear, step, and oscillating functions."><label/><tgroup cols="2">

          <tbody>
          <row><entry>
          <para id="para-00002">
            <media alt="A graph with a line in quadrant I that has a negative slope, intercepting the y-axis and x-axis.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_02_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para></entry>
          <entry>
          <para id="para-00003">
            <media alt="A step function graph with horizontal segments increasing from left to right. Each step starts with a closed black dot and ends with an open white dot, starting at the origin and extending rightward.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_02_02.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para></entry></row>
          <row><entry>
          <para id="para-00004">
            <media alt="A graph with horizontal and vertical axes. The horizontal line starts at a y-intercept and extends right, representing a constant value. Both axes have arrows indicating positive directions.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_02_03.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para></entry>
          <entry>
          <para id="para-00005">
            <media alt="A graph showing a wave with increasing and then decreasing amplitude, centered along the horizontal axis. Both vertical and horizontal axes are drawn, with the origin marked.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_02_04.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para></entry></row>
          </tbody></tgroup></table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Which graph does not belong? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Any answer could be correct.</para>

          <para id="para-00009">Graph A doesn’t belong because:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>It is the only graph that has a positive vertical intercept and a positive horizontal intercept.</item>
            <item>It is the only one that has a constant, negative rate of change.</item>
          </list>

          <para id="para-00010">Graph B doesn’t belong because:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>It is the only graph that is not a continuous curve and has multiple pieces. </item>
            <item>It is the only one that shows both points and lines.</item>
          </list>

          <para id="para-00011">Graph C doesn’t belong because:</para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item>It is the only graph that has a constant <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-value.</item>
          </list>

          <para id="para-00012">Graph D doesn’t belong because:</para>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item>It is the only graph whose <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-value increases and decreases multiple times. </item>
            <item>It is the only graph that is not a simple line or curve and that has wave-like features. </item>
          </list>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
